# Getting Started with Loom

This tutorial gets you from a clean machine to a working Loom CLI session.

<Callout variant="info" title="What you will complete">
	You will install `loom`, authenticate with a server, start a thread, and resume it from history.
</Callout>

## Prerequisites

- A Loom server URL (for example `https://loom.ghuntley.com`)
- A user account on that server
- A terminal environment with Rust/Nix available

## 1. Install the CLI

Pick one path.

<Steps>
	<Step title="Build with Nix (recommended in this repository)">

```bash
nix build .#loom-cli-c2n
./result/bin/loom version
```

	</Step>
	<Step title="Build with Cargo for active development">

```bash
cargo build -p loom-cli
./target/debug/loom version
```

	</Step>
</Steps>

## 2. Authenticate

Login stores credentials for the configured server URL.

```bash
loom --server-url https://loom.ghuntley.com login
```

To verify auth state, run a command that requires server access, such as listing weavers:

```bash
loom --server-url https://loom.ghuntley.com weaver ps
```

## 3. Start your first interactive thread

Running `loom` with no subcommand creates a new thread and enters the REPL.

```bash
loom --server-url https://loom.ghuntley.com
```

Type your request, then exit with `Ctrl+C` when finished.

## 4. Inspect and resume thread history

```bash
loom list
loom resume
loom resume <thread-id>
```

- `loom list` shows known threads.
- `loom resume` reopens the most recent thread.
- `loom resume <thread-id>` resumes a specific thread.

<Callout variant="tip" title="Private local-only sessions">
	Use `loom private` when the session must never sync to the server.
</Callout>

